WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.712
Refraction is one of the richest ideas in the wave unit because several facts have to stay linked at the same time.

2
00:00:05.712 --> 00:00:11.425
When a wave crosses into a new medium, its speed can change.

3
00:00:11.425 --> 00:00:17.138
Because different parts of the front enter at slightly different times, the whole front can turn.

4
00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:22.850
That turning is refraction.

5
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.562
At the same time, the source that created the wave usually stays the same, so the frequency remains fixed across the boundary.

6
00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:34.275
If the speed changes while the frequency stays fixed, the wavelength must change as well.

7
00:00:34.275 --> 00:00:39.988
The worked example will only feel easy if all four of those ideas stay connected rather than being learned one by one and then forgotten.

8
00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:45.700
Keep this takeaway in view: Refraction is best understood as one linked system: speed change, fixed frequency, wavelength change, and bend direction.

9
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.412
Cause means speed change.

10
00:00:51.412 --> 00:00:57.125
A new medium can make the wave travel faster or slower.

11
00:00:57.125 --> 00:01:02.837
Visible result means turning front.

12
00:01:02.837 --> 00:01:08.550
One side of the front changes pace first, so the whole wave bends.

13
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:14.847
This diagram is excellent because it keeps the before-and-after states visible together.

14
00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:21.143
In the first medium, the wave has one speed and one wavelength.

15
00:01:21.143 --> 00:01:27.440
In the second medium, the wave can have a different speed and therefore a different wavelength.

16
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:33.737
The frequency is not redrawn as a separate arrow, yet it is still part of the logic because the same source is driving the wave.

17
00:01:33.737 --> 00:01:40.033
The turning happens because one side of the wavefront meets the new medium first and changes pace first.

18
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:46.330
That unequal timing rotates the front and changes the direction of travel.

19
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:52.627
Keep this takeaway in view: The bend direction only makes sense when the speed change is read at the same time.

20
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:58.923
Medium A: Start with the original speed and wavelength.

21
00:01:58.923 --> 00:02:05.220
Medium B: Read the new speed and the new wavelength after the boundary.

22
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:09.540
Imagine a straight line of runners or carts moving together toward a muddy region.

23
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:13.860
If the left side reaches the mud first, that side slows down first while the other side is still moving faster on firm ground.

24
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.180
The line no longer stays straight in the same direction.

25
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.500
It pivots and turns.

26
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:26.820
That is the heart of refraction.

27
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:31.140
The wavefront does not turn because it wants to.

28
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.460
It turns because one part changes speed first.

29
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.780
The analogy also helps with bend direction.

30
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.100
Entering a slower medium bends the path toward the normal.

31
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.420
Entering a faster medium bends the path away from the normal.

32
00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.740
Keep this takeaway in view: A front turns because different parts of it change pace at different times.

33
00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.060
Slower region means one side lags.

34
00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.380
That delayed side makes the line pivot toward the normal.

35
00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:05.700
Faster region means one side races ahead.

36
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.020
That side makes the line pivot away from the normal.

37
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.769
The key relationship still comes from v equals f times lambda, but refraction adds a new condition.

38
00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:19.519
The source keeps the frequency fixed across the boundary.

39
00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:24.268
That means if the speed changes in the new medium, the wavelength must change with it.

40
00:03:24.268 --> 00:03:29.018
A slower medium gives a shorter wavelength.

41
00:03:29.018 --> 00:03:33.767
A faster medium gives a longer wavelength.

42
00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:38.516
The bend direction follows the same logic.

43
00:03:38.516 --> 00:03:43.266
Slower means toward the normal.

44
00:03:43.266 --> 00:03:48.015
Faster means away from the normal.

45
00:03:48.015 --> 00:03:52.765
This is why refraction answers feel richer than plain wave-equation questions.

46
00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:57.514
You are not just calculating.

47
00:03:57.514 --> 00:04:02.264
You are linking geometry and wave quantities together.

48
00:04:02.264 --> 00:04:07.013
Keep this takeaway in view: Refraction depends on keeping one fixed quantity and two changing quantities in view at the same time.

49
00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:11.762
Source rule: The launch rate from the source does not suddenly change at the boundary.

50
00:04:11.762 --> 00:04:16.512
Wave equation: If v changes while f stays fixed, lambda must change too.

51
00:04:16.512 --> 00:04:21.261
v = f x lambda in each medium.

52
00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:26.011
frequency stays constant across the boundary.

53
00:04:26.011 --> 00:04:30.760
If the new medium is faster, the wavelength becomes longer and the bend is away from the normal.

54
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.836
Many students try to memorize bend direction without a cause.

55
00:04:35.836 --> 00:04:40.912
A better pattern is this.

56
00:04:40.912 --> 00:04:45.988
If the new medium is slower, the wave bends toward the normal.

57
00:04:45.988 --> 00:04:51.065
If the new medium is faster, the wave bends away from the normal.

58
00:04:51.065 --> 00:04:56.141
If the wave meets the boundary head-on, there is no sideways turning even though the speed and wavelength can still change.

59
00:04:56.141 --> 00:05:01.217
That three-case structure is more dependable than memorization because each case is tied to a physical reason.

60
00:05:01.217 --> 00:05:06.293
It also helps you interpret diagrams even when no numbers are given.

61
00:05:06.293 --> 00:05:11.369
Keep this takeaway in view: Bend direction is not a separate fact.

62
00:05:11.369 --> 00:05:16.445
It is a visible consequence of the speed change.

63
00:05:16.445 --> 00:05:21.522
Slower medium means toward normal.

64
00:05:21.522 --> 00:05:26.598
The front side that enters first slows first, turning the path inward.

65
00:05:26.598 --> 00:05:31.674
Faster medium means away from normal.

66
00:05:31.674 --> 00:05:36.750
The front side that enters first speeds ahead, turning the path outward.

67
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.545
Try four checks.

68
00:05:40.545 --> 00:05:44.341
What actually causes refraction?

69
00:05:44.341 --> 00:05:48.136
A change in wave speed in a new medium.

70
00:05:48.136 --> 00:05:51.931
Which quantity usually stays fixed at the boundary?

71
00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:55.727
Frequency, because the source still sets it.

72
00:05:55.727 --> 00:05:59.522
If the new medium is slower, what happens to wavelength?

73
00:05:59.522 --> 00:06:03.317
It becomes shorter.

74
00:06:03.317 --> 00:06:07.113
And which way does the wave bend in that slower case?

75
00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:10.908
Toward the normal.

76
00:06:10.908 --> 00:06:14.703
Those four checks are the backbone of the lesson.

77
00:06:14.703 --> 00:06:18.499
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: refraction is now one connected story rather than several loose facts.

78
00:06:18.499 --> 00:06:22.294
Cause means speed change.

79
00:06:22.294 --> 00:06:26.089
Refraction begins with a new medium changing the wave speed.

80
00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:29.885
Constant means frequency.

81
00:06:29.885 --> 00:06:33.680
The source still controls the launch rate on both sides of the boundary.

82
00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:39.862
You are ready for the worked example now.

83
00:06:39.862 --> 00:06:46.044
Begin by identifying whether the second medium is slower or faster than the first.

84
00:06:46.044 --> 00:06:52.226
That tells you the bend direction immediately.

85
00:06:52.226 --> 00:06:58.409
Next state that the source keeps the frequency fixed across the boundary.

86
00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:04.591
Then use the wave equation to explain how the wavelength changes with the new speed.

87
00:07:04.591 --> 00:07:10.773
If the example is qualitative, tie the turn to one side of the front entering first.

88
00:07:10.773 --> 00:07:16.955
If the example is numerical, calculate the new wavelength or speed carefully while keeping the same frequency.

89
00:07:16.955 --> 00:07:23.137
That method keeps the geometry and the arithmetic working together instead of fighting each other.

90
00:07:23.137 --> 00:07:29.319
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: refraction now means speed change, fixed frequency, new wavelength, and predictable bend direction.

91
00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:35.501
Step 1: Compare the two media and predict toward-normal or away-from-normal bending.

92
00:07:35.501 --> 00:07:41.684
Step 2: State that the source keeps the frequency fixed across the boundary.

93
00:07:41.684 --> 00:07:47.866
decide whether the new medium is slower or faster.

94
00:07:47.866 --> 00:07:54.048
keep frequency fixed because the source is unchanged.

95
00:07:54.048 --> 00:08:00.230
Strong refraction answers always separate what stays constant from what changes at the boundary.
